@Tutorial(time: 60, projectFiles: "3d_squash_the_creeps_starter.zip") {
    @Intro(title: "Your first 3D game") {
        In this step-by-step tutorial series, you will create your first complete 3D
        game with Xogot. By the end of the series, you will have a simple yet finished
        project of your own like the animated gif below.

        @Image(source: squash-the-creeps-final.gif, alt: "Animated gif of Save the Axolotl: Squash the Snakes running")

        The game we'll code here is **Save the Axolotl: Squash the Snakes**. It's
        similar to [Your first 2D game](https://docs.xogot.com/documentation/tutorials/your-first-2d-game),
        with a twist: you can now jump and your goal is to squash the snakes. This way,
        you will both **recognize patterns** you learned in the previous tutorial and
        **build upon them** with new code and features.

        You will learn to:

        - Work with 3D coordinates with a jumping mechanic.
        - Use kinematic bodies to move 3D characters and detect when and how they collide.
        - Use physics layers and a group to detect interactions with specific entities.
        - Code basic procedural gameplay by instancing monsters at regular time intervals.
        - Design a movement animation and change its speed at runtime.
        - Draw a user interface on a 3D game.

        And more.

        This tutorial is for beginners who followed the complete getting started series.
        We'll start slow with detailed instructions and shorten them as we do similar
        steps. If you're an experienced programmer, you can browse the complete demo's
        source code here:

        - [Save the Axolotl: Squash the Snakes source code (GDScript)](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-demo-projects/tree/master/3d/squash%5Fthe%5Fcreeps)

        > Note: You can follow this series without having done the 2D one. However, if
        > you're new to game development, we recommend you to start with 2D. 3D game
        > code is always more complex and the 2D series will give you foundations to
        > follow along more comfortably.

        We prepared some game assets so we can jump straight to the code. You can
        download them here:
        [Save the Axolotl: Squash the Snakes assets](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-docs-project-starters/releases/download/latest-4.x/3d%5Fsquash%5Fthe%5Fcreeps%5Fstarter.zip).

        We will first work on a basic prototype for the player's movement, then import
        the player's 3D model. We will then add the monsters that we'll spawn randomly
        around the screen. After that, we'll add the jump and the hit box and hurt box
        mechanics for squashing monsters and dying. We'll then add the score and the
        retry screen, and wrap up by refining the game with some nice character
        animations.
    }
    @Section(title: "Contents") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            This tutorial is divided into the following lessons:

            - [Setting up the game area](doc:01.game_setup)
            - [Player scene and input actions](doc:02.player_input)
            - [Importing the player model](doc:03.importing_player_model)
            - [Moving the player with code](doc:04.player_movement_code)
            - [Designing the mob scene](doc:05.mob_scene)
            - [Spawning monsters](doc:06.spawning_mobs)
            - [Jumping](doc:07.jump)
            - [Hit boxes](doc:08.hit_boxes)
            - [Hurt boxes](doc:09.hurt_boxes)
            - [Score and replay](doc:10.score_and_replay)
            - [Character animation](doc:11.adding_animations)
            - [Going further](doc:going_further)

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
        }
    }
}
